1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:09,760
In the 1950s, four sisters in central Mexico ran some of the most feared brothels in the country. People called them Las Poquianchis.

2
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:20,770
Delfina, Carmen, María Luisa and María de Jesús González Valenzuela grew up in Jalisco, daughters of a violent, hard-drinking small-town policeman.

3
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:26,790
When he shot a man, the family fled, changed its surname, and settled in Guanajuato.

4
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:38,840
Delfina, the eldest, opened their first bar. A back room soon made more money than the bar itself. Before long, they ran brothels across Jalisco, Guanajuato, and Querétaro.

5
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:45,890
Their trap was simple: a newspaper ad. Housemaid wanted. Poor young women answered it… and never came home.

6
00:00:45,910 --> 00:00:55,610
Inside, they were locked in, forced to work, and beaten. Debts they could never repay kept them there. Those who got sick, or became inconvenient, disappeared.

7
00:00:55,630 --> 00:01:04,150
A former army captain, Delfina's partner, guarded the doors. And according to later accounts, some local officials looked the other way.

8
00:01:04,170 --> 00:01:11,620
In 1964, a young woman named Catalina Ortega escaped, and reached the police in León.

9
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:17,610
Officers went to the sisters' ranch near San Francisco del Rincón… and started digging.

10
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:27,510
Delfina and María de Jesús were arrested. María Luisa fled at the last moment. The tabloid press splashed the story across Mexico.

11
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:38,760
Reports claimed ninety-one bodies: women, men, even unborn children. Historians still argue over the true number. Some say only eight victims were ever confirmed.

12
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:46,060
The surviving sisters were each sentenced to forty years in prison. Carmen had died of cancer years before.

13
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:59,470
Delfina died in prison in 1968, in a strange accident. María Luisa died behind bars in 1984. María de Jesús was eventually released… and vanished from public view.

14
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:11,070
Mexico never stopped retelling the story: in Felipe Cazals' 1976 film, in Jorge Ibargüengoitia's novel Las Muertas, and in a 2025 series.

15
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:19,518
For more than a decade, everyone knew what those houses were. Nobody asked where the girls went. This is Plot Twist with A&R.

